  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 247 AM CST Sat Nov 28 2026\n\nNear-zonal flow aloft is expected to persist Monday through Tuesday \nnight, with subtle upper-level ridging developing ahead of a potent \nstorm system moving into the southwestern CONUS. Temperatures will \ntrend from near to slightly above seasonal averages Monday to\nabove average readings Tuesday and Wednesday. Highs Tuesday \ncould approach 60 degrees across southwest Nebraska, with 50s \nelsewhere. Highs Wednesday will generally reach the middle 50s \nacross most of the area.\n\nCyclogenesis is expected to get underway across southeast Colorado \nand the Texas/Oklahoma Panhandles Wednesday as the upper-level \nshortwave approaches. Increasing southeasterly low-level flow across \nthe southern Plains will promote northward moisture transport and a \ngradual return of Gulf moisture. Surface dewpoints are expected to \nclimb into the lower 50s as far north as southern Kansas by \nWednesday evening. Moisture will continue to advect northward, \npotentially reaching western and north-central Nebraska by Thursday \nevening, with ENS precipitable water values exceeding one-half inch.\n\nAs the shortwave ejects onto the Plains Thursday, considerable \ndifferences remain between the deterministic ECMWF and GFS solutions \nregarding the track of the system. The ECMWF continues to favor a \nmore northern track, bringing precipitation to much of western and \nnorth-central Nebraska, while the GFS maintains a more southern \ntrajectory across the southern Plains, keeping the bulk of the \nprecipitation south of the forecast area. Ensemble guidance \ncontinues to lean toward the wetter ECMWF solution, with ENS \nprobabilities of at least a tenth of an inch of liquid precipitation\nexceeding 50% along and southeast of an O'Neill to Thedford to \nSidney line. The GEFS is less bullish but still indicates \nprobabilities around 30% for at least a tenth of an inch across \nareas southeast of the aforementioned line.\n\nRegarding snowfall potential, the lack of a substantial intrusion of \ncolder Canadian air on the backside of the system should limit \naccumulation potential. At this time, any snowfall appears likely to \nremain relatively light, with the ENS indicating approximately a 20% \nchance of an inch or more across the western Sandhills. There \nremains considerable uncertainty in the track and evolution of this \nsystem, and forecast details will continue to be refined as \nadditional model guidance becomes available.\n\nOtherwise, temperatures will trend back toward seasonal averages \nThursday and Friday as increasing cloud cover and precipitation \npotential arrive Thursday, followed by cooler air filtering \ninto the region behind the system Friday.",
